  "mechaber/part-001.txt": {
    "1:main": "The law of milah on Shabbat. And in it 10 seifim: They do all needs of milah on Shabbat: they circumcise, and perform priyah, and perform metzitzah, and place upon it cumin powder:",
    "2:main": "All the time he has not removed his hand from the milah — he returns even for tzitzin that are not impeding; he removed his hand — he returns only for tzitzin that impede; and these are what impede: flesh that covers, even the majority of the height of the atarah in one place:",
    "3:main": "An eight-day-old: if his hair and nails finished [growing] — they circumcise him on Shabbat; and if they did not finish — even if he is doubtful seven-day versus eight-day — they do not circumcise him, and needless to say a definite eight-day-old; and if he is a definite seven-day-old — even if his hair and nails did not finish — they circumcise him [and see in Yoreh De'ah siman 266]:",
    "4:main": "A milah not at its time does not override Shabbat:",
    "5:main": "An androgynous; and one born at twilight; and one born already circumcised; and one born by caesarean section; and a non-Jew who gave birth and afterward converted; and one who has two arlot — their milah does not override Shabbat:",
    "6:main": "Accessories for milah that it is possible to prepare from erev Shabbat — do not override Shabbat; therefore if he did not bring a scalpel for milah from erev Shabbat — he shall not bring it on Shabbat, even in a place where there is only a d'rabbanan prohibition, for the Rabbis established their words in place of kares; and to tell a non-Jew to do it — if it is something that if an Israelite did it there is only d'rabbanan prohibition — he tells a non-Jew and he does it; and if it is something forbidden for an Israelite from the Torah — he shall not tell a non-Jew to do it (and see above siman 307):",
    "7:main": "If he did not have ground cumin from erev Shabbat — he shall not grind it except by chewing with his teeth; if he did not establish an eruv yesterday for wine and oil to apply upon them — he shall not mix them today, but shall apply each one separately:",
    "8:main": "They do not make for it a shirt, but wrap upon it a rag; and if he does not have — he wraps upon his finger as a way of wearing a garment to change from the way of carrying on a weekday, and brings through another courtyard even if they did not establish an eruv together:",
    "9:main": "In the time of the sages of the gemara, if they did not bathe the infant before milah and after milah and on the third day of milah in hot water — it was dangerous; therefore it was necessary to write its law when it occurs on Shabbat; but nowadays they do not practice bathing at all, and its law is to bathe on Shabbat if they wish like the law of bathing for any person: {Rama: And in these countries they practice bathing him before milah in hot water heated from yesterday, and after milah at Motzei Shabbat; and so if the third day of his milah was on Shabbat and they see there is need to bathe him — they prepare hot water while it is still day and bathe him on Shabbat; and all this is from presumption; but if they see there is reason to fear danger if they do not bathe him after milah — certainly permitted to bathe him and desecrate Shabbat for him like other illnesses with danger:}",
    "10:main": "A person who never circumcised — he shall not circumcise on Shabbat; but if he already circumcised once — permitted to circumcise on Shabbat, even if he is the father of the child: {Rama: And see further laws of milah Tur Yoreh De'ah siman 266:}"
  },
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt": {
    "5:א": "Twilight. See Magen Avraham who raised that if he was born a quarter-hour before nightfall it is a safek, and if born before that they circumcise on the sixth day; and if born on Shabbat a quarter-hour before night they circumcise on Shabbat erev Shabbat; and in Radbaz siman 282 he wrote: an infant born on erev Shabbat after sunset of the sun before three medium stars appeared — if the matter is doubtful whether he was born before the end of sunset or after the end of sunset and before three stars appeared — since there are three doubts, it is deferred to the tenth [day] erev Shabbat; and see at the end of Sefer Darkei Noam, large pamphlet from Milchamot Mitzvah, with all details of laws and many distinctions at length. And see in responsa Bach siman 154. And Maharam Schick siman 41:",
    "5:ב": "When he is already circumcised. Requires study why they do not practice now to examine afterward. And it appears to me as Bach wrote in Yoreh De'ah siman 264 that one must see him circumcised even not at the time of erection on erev Shabbat; and likewise if he was not seen circumcised not at the time of erection — they circumcise on Shabbat. And requires study whether one may rely on this to desecrate severe Shabbat (Magen Avraham). (And see in Shach Yoreh De'ah end of siman 264 and see Yoreh De'ah siman 263 that if there is a pressed foreskin, meaning the skin is very stuck to the flesh — it overrides Shabbat; and in responsum Mahari Beit siman 89 he wrote that if there are tzitzin impeding the milah — it overrides; and in Agudah chapter R' Eliezer of Milah he wrote: one born circumcised on Shabbat — good to circumcise him on the day after, from which it implies he holds there is no prohibition when circumcising on Shabbat; but requires study, for the gemara is arranged in Shabbos 135 that it does not override Shabbat, and it is a great stretch to explain Agudah on another matter on erev Shabbat; and Rif is in doubt on it, and so Maharil — see in Sefer Eliyah Rabbah):",
    "6:א": "Scalpel. See what is written in siman 310 s.k. 3 whether one may move the scalpel after milah, and see Magen Avraham:",
    "6:ב": "He tells a non-Jew. See siman 266 seif 6 regarding the law of carrying out the infant, and see Magen Avraham:",
    "7:_": "He shall not mix them. Beit Yosef wrote that for other sick people too it is forbidden to mix on erev Shabbat:",
    "9:_": "And they bathe. And he shall not wring a sheet, for it is like laundering; or a non-Jew puts the sheet into the water; and if the hot water spilled — he tells a non-Jew to bring water heated for the non-Jew, and he shall not tell a non-Jew to heat for him — Beit Yosef wrote in Yoreh De'ah siman 267 in name of Rashbetz that an infant who was ill and was not circumcised at its time and recovered — they do not circumcise him on the fifth day, lest they need to desecrate Shabbat on the third day of his milah; and also on the sixth day they practice prohibition on erev Shabbat; and see there Shach and Taz; and see in Knesset HaGedolah. And Magen Avraham wrote: and it appears to me that in our time when they do not bathe him — there is no concern for this, for it is not common at all that they would desecrate Shabbat for him; therefore one should not be lax in a mitzvah matter on erev Shabbat:",
    "10:_": "He shall not circumcise. And it appears to me that likewise if he circumcised but never performed priyah — forbidden to perform priyah on Shabbat; and one reason for them — Magen Avraham. And see in responsum Chatam Sofer siman 158. A mohel who comes to circumcise on Shabbat and says he already circumcised once on a weekday — he is believed and need not bring proof. Shemuel is believed siman 19. And see in Yoreh De'ah siman 266 that two mohalim should not circumcise one milah on Shabbat — one circumcises and one performs priyah — rather the one who circumcises himself performs priyah; and see there Taz s.k. 11. And they practice according to the ruling of Shulchan Aruch; and see Dvar Shemuel siman 64:"
  },
